 "steps": [
  {
   "say": "This is your ESP32 DevKit, plugged into a breadboard. On the right-hand header, the 12th pin down is D2, which is GPIO 2, and two pins further down is GND.",
   "scene": [
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "hr#12"
    },
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "hr#14"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Run a jumper from D2 into the breadboard. Then put a 220 ohm resistor from that row into a new row, to limit the current.",
   "scene": [
    {
     "do": "show",
     "target": "w1"
    },
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "res"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Put the LED's long leg, the positive side, in the same row as the resistor's other end. The short leg goes into a different row.",
   "scene": [
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "anode"
    },
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "cathode"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "say": "Finally, wire the short leg's row to GND on the ESP32. Set GPIO 2 to HIGH in your code and the LED lights up.",
   "scene": [
    {
     "do": "show",
     "target": "w2"
    },
    {
     "along": "w1",
     "color": "#ffd000",
     "do": "flow"
    },
    {
     "do": "color",
     "target": "led",
     "color": "#ff6060"
    },
    {
     "do": "highlight",
     "target": "led"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
